WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:00.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  taliban insurgents are rapidly making  ground in afghanistan  thousands have died and hundreds of  thousands more are homeless  so what happens now  the taliban believes in an extreme  version of islamic law when the group  was in power in afghanistan between 1996  and 2001  girls education beyond the age of 10 was  outlawed  public executions were common as were  stonings  television was banned and religious  dress codes were strictly enforced  after u.s troops arrived in 2001 in  response to the 911 terror attacks and  the taliban was ousted rules were  relaxed the number of girls in education  went up from 9 000 in 1999 to three and  a half million today  we have a lot of gain and achievement uh  we have women in different parts of of  the society women politicians women  economists women and health services and  everywhere  but now u.s british and other nato  troops have withdrawn from the country  afghan leaders  have to come together  we lost  thousands of american personnel  they've got to fight for themselves  and the taliban's influence has grown  once again  women are already seeing their freedoms  curbed and human rights violations are  on the rise  um  [Music]  the uk un and us say that they won't  recognize the taliban as a legitimate  government if it arrives by force we  will not accept a military takeover of  afghanistan  or return of the taliban's islamic  emirate  but the chinese government has already  held some talks with the group afraid  and russia has traditionally shown an  interest in the country so could be  another ally  in fact russia was the first country to  publicly engage with the taliban after  u.s troops withdrew early this year  the kremlin wants to protect its borders  from islamic extremism by allying with  the taliban against al-qaeda an islamic  state  iran's ties with the taliban also seem  to be growing  back in january senior officials in  tehran received a taliban delegation to  discuss relations between the two  countries  and india which has previously refused  to open communication channels with the  group has now confirmed it has been  talking to taliban officials  nearly half of the afghan population  currently requires humanitarian  assistance according to the world health  organization including 3.1 million  children  the world health organization said it is  struggling to get supplies in and  medical centers have been attacked by  the taliban  shipments of coveted vaccines through  the kovac scheme are still being  delivered the taliban says that it  supports the government's immunization  drive and officials hope that taliban  soldiers will leave vaccination centers  unharmed  the taliban themselves however brutal  have never been a threat outside of  afghanistan but under their previous  rule al-qaeda was free to plan the 9 11  attacks from inside the country they  gave safe harbor to osama bin laden  as part of the peace deal negotiated  with president trump last year the  taliban said it wouldn't allow terrorist  groups to shelter in the country  but if it does take power it's likely  that that promise will be broken  and so as the taliban's control over  afghanistan grows the stakes for the  country and the world  are high

